  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypostomus-sertanejo-2/",
      "html": "<p>Hypostomus sertanejo becomes about 20-25 cm long. It is a typical representative of its genus and a diligent algae exterminator. The nice juvenile markings with large white spots remain for a long time, even about 10 cm long specimens still show them. In fully adult fish the spots are much smaller.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypostomus-sertanejo-2/",
      "html": "<p>Rio Jaguaribe (Ceará State, Brazil). The species was described scientifically only in 2017, although 19 specimens were found in the scientific collection of the Academy of Natural Sciences of Philadelphia, U.S.A., collected and deposited there by R. von Ihering in about 1936.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypostomus-sertanejo-2/",
      "html": "<p>In nature, the fish lives in clear water, the bottom consists of boulders and sand, sometimes with underwater vegetation. Fish species with which it lives together in nature include Parotocinclus jumbo (LDA25), Moenkhausia costae and Tetragonopterus argenteus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypostomus-sertanejo-2/",
      "html": "<p>The young fish are very lively and are also very active during the day in the aquarium. Among themselves they are peaceful, and no aggressive behaviour towards other fish is to be expected.</p>"
    }
  ],
